<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">István Bethlen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count István Bethlen was prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1921 to 1931. A Transylvanian aristocrat, he stabilised the post-war kingdom under Regent Horthy, restored open voting in the countryside, obtained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> loan and sought allies, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, for revising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a>. Arrested by Soviet forces in 1945, he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istvan-bethlen.jpg" alt="István Bethlen" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bethlen_Istv%C3%A1n_1930s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bethlen was born on 8 October 1874, the only son of Count István Bethlen de Bethlen and Countess Ilona Teleki de Szék, with two elder sisters. His family was an old noble house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. He was elected to the Hungarian Parliament as a liberal in 1901 and married his distant cousin, the author Countess Margarete Bethlen de Bethlen, on 27 June 1901. They had three sons.</p><p>The British historian C. A. Macartney described him as a poor public speaker, very reserved, with the assurance of a man whose family had been obeyed for centuries. He spoke Hungarian, English, French, German and Romanian. Macartney called him a conservative in the truest sense, who believed in the right of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> basin through his own class.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the attempt of King Charles IV to return to the throne in 1921, Horthy asked Bethlen to form a strong government. Bethlen disliked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> and, though he wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to remain a monarchy, opposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> restoration. Horthy appointed him as a compromise: as a Calvinist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> he was expected to follow a revisionist foreign policy, which suited the radicals, while his standing among the great aristocratic families satisfied the conservatives. He was prime minister until 1931.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 11 October 1932 anti-Jewish demonstrations broke out at Hungarian universities, with Christian students complaining that Jewish students exceeded the quota of the numerus clausus law of 1920. Gömbös and his education minister Bálint Hóman supported the protests, and Gömbös used them to charge that Bethlen had failed to enforce the law during his decade in office.</p><p>Bethlen was increasingly sidelined, but he stood out as one of the few Hungarian voices opposed to an alliance with Nazi Germany. He objected that the Germans wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to remain agrarian and supply them with cheap food and raw materials, and expected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “István Bethlen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prime Minister <b>István Bethlen</b>'s government stabilised the currency with large foreign loans and introduced the pengő in 1927, and industry, farming and trade grew for most of the 1920s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gornești, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.67,24.65&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Gornești on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5130 523 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of István Bethlen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.67,24.65&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
